<PageHeader Eyebrow="LESSON C01 / ARCHITECTURE & WORKFLOW" Title="Follow every request." Accent="Understand every step."
            Intro="Trace a conversation from the student to the remote session and back. This UML sequence diagram follows the actual C# / Blazor implementation." />

<section id="prerequisites" class="student-section" aria-labelledby="prerequisites-title">
    <div class="student-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">STEP 0 / PREPARE YOUR WORKSPACE</p>
        <h2 id="prerequisites-title">Before the workflow: prepare an agent and environment</h2>
        <p>A session needs both an agent ID and an environment ID. After building the app and storing the API key in step 1, reuse suitable existing resource IDs and skip steps 2–3. Create only the resources you are missing; each creation command makes a new remote resource.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="learning-outcome"><strong>Setup → conversation:</strong> store the API key → create or reuse an agent → create or reuse an environment → save both IDs → start the app → create a session.</p>
    <ol id="prerequisite-steps" class="run-steps">
        <li><div class="step-content">
            <h3>Build the app and store the API key</h3>
            <p>Open PowerShell in <code>lesson-c01/lesson-c01-csharp-app</code>, the folder containing <code>SessionLab.slnx</code>. Build once, then store your key as a .NET user secret:</p>
            <pre><code class="language-powershell">dotnet build
dotnet user-secrets set ANTHROPIC_API_KEY your_api_key --project src/SessionLab</code></pre>
            <p>Use your real key; the workspace must have Managed Agents access. The same configuration feeds both the web app and the setup commands, which check for a missing key before creating resources.</p>
            <CodeExample Snippet="Snippets.SetupDispatch" />
            <CodeExample Snippet="Snippets.SetupKeyCheck" />
        </div></li>
        <li><div class="step-content">
            <h3>Create the agent configuration</h3>
            <pre><code class="language-powershell">dotnet run --project src/SessionLab -- create-agent</code></pre>
            <p>This runs <code>CreateAgentAsync</code> in <code>Setup/SetupCommands.cs</code>. The agent configuration defines the model, instructions, and available tools; it is not a conversation. The command prints <code>CLAUDE_MANAGED_AGENT_ID=...</code>. Save that returned ID.</p>
            <p>The model shown below is the command's configured choice. If your workspace rejects it, select a model supported by that workspace before retrying.</p>
            <CodeExample Snippet="Snippets.CreateAgent" />
        </div></li>
        <li><div class="step-content">
            <h3>Create the environment configuration</h3>
            <pre><code class="language-powershell">dotnet run --project src/SessionLab -- create-environment</code></pre>
            <p>This runs <code>CreateEnvironmentAsync</code>. It creates a reusable cloud environment configuration with limited networking and package-manager access enabled. The command prints <code>CLAUDE_MANAGED_ENVIRONMENT_ID=...</code>. Save that returned ID.</p>
            <p>This setup request creates a configuration; it does not create the conversation or demonstrate a command running in a session workspace.</p>
            <CodeExample Snippet="Snippets.CreateEnvironment" />
        </div></li>
        <li><div class="step-content">
            <h3>Save both IDs, then start the application</h3>
            <p>Store the printed IDs next to your API key. Replace the example values with your own:</p>
            <pre><code class="language-powershell">dotnet user-secrets set CLAUDE_MANAGED_AGENT_ID your_agent_id --project src/SessionLab
dotnet user-secrets set CLAUDE_MANAGED_ENVIRONMENT_ID your_environment_id --project src/SessionLab
dotnet run --project src/SessionLab</code></pre>
            <p>If the server was already running, stop it with <strong>Ctrl+C</strong> and start it again to reload the configuration. Open <code>http://127.0.0.1:3000</code> and click <strong>New session</strong>. The runtime workflow below now uses the saved agent and environment IDs.</p>
            <CodeExample Snippet="Snippets.ClientRegistration" />
            <p class="step-note"><strong>For the optional pending-action exercise:</strong> <code>dotnet run --project src/SessionLab -- create-pending-demo-agent</code> creates a separate agent with a custom tool and confirmation policy. Note your original agent ID before replacing it; reuse the environment ID and restart the server. This extra agent is not required for an ordinary conversation.</p>
        </div></li>
    </ol>
